WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.289 --> 00:00:07.980
(dramatic music)

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.830
- [Narrator] Minneapolis is bracing

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.890
for the high profile
trial of Derek Chauvin,

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.700
the former police officer
charged with second degree murder

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.072
and second degree manslaughter
in the death of George Floyd.

00:00:18.072 --> 00:00:18.905
- You're resisting arrest
- I cannot breath

00:00:18.905 --> 00:00:21.671
- [Unseen Female] Are you having fun?

00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:23.810
- [Unseen Male] You're a
grown, you're a tough guy.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.200
- Video of the incident

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.820
in which Chauvin is seen
kneeling on Floyd's neck,

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.854
While, Floyd repeated
that he couldn't breathe

00:00:29.854 --> 00:00:33.510
sparked worldwide protests and
launched a national reckoning

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.820
over police brutality against black people

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.133
in the United States.

00:00:38.944 --> 00:00:43.652
- Say his name! George
Floyd! (people chanting)

00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:45.030
(somber music)

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.630
The trial will be live streamed

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.950
and authorities expect it to
inflame tensions once again.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.320
More than 3000 law enforcement officers

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.820
and national guard troops could be

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.200
on call as the jury deliberates.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.410
- This is a difficult
case in so many ways.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.340
People around the world
have seen this footage,

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.270
but people in Minneapolis
have seen the footage

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.530
and what it's done to the city.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.392
I mean, you had riots

00:01:06.392 --> 00:01:09.840
a billion dollars worth
of damage to businesses.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.780
1500 businesses were
somehow impacted by this.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.640
- We recognize that
throughout the trial itself

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.240
people will want to gather,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.800
they'll want to protest.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.060
They'll want to express
their first amendment rights.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.590
And we want to enable them to
do so safely and peacefully.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.810
- [Narrator] Due to concerns
surrounding COVID-19

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.210
and social distancing

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.970
Chauvin, will be tried alone.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:35.242
His lawyer has made filings
indicating he's not guilty.

00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:36.075
(suspenseful music)

00:01:36.075 --> 00:01:38.330
So, what kind of evidence
will be presented?

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.720
Who might testify?

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.740
And what is the bar for conviction?

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.518
Here are three things to
know about Chauvin's trial.

00:01:44.518 --> 00:01:48.480
First video evidence is expected
to play a significant role.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.120
The prosecution is likely to seize

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.100
on the footage that shows Chauvin kneeling

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.040
on Floyd's neck for around eight minutes.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.540
As prosecutors have pointed out previously

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.940
Chauvin continued to
kneel on Floyd's neck,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.140
for about four minutes

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.668
after an officer said
Floyd was passing out

00:02:03.668 --> 00:02:06.860
and for two and a half minutes
after another officer said,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.329
Floyd did not have a pulse.

00:02:08.329 --> 00:02:09.355
- [Policeman Kneeling] Relax!

00:02:09.355 --> 00:02:11.310
- [Man On Ground] Man I
can't breath (mumbles)

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.520
- I think those last

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.950
minutes after he's already

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.730
no longer not only not moving,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.040
but they can't even find a pulse,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.160
how the jury is gonna react
to seeing that again in court

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.040
is probably one of the
big moments of the trial.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.530
- [Narrator] The defense may also use

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.710
video evidence to bolster its case.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.070
Though, it is likely to focus

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.163
on what happened before
those final minutes.

00:02:34.163 --> 00:02:34.996
- [Policeman] You got 'em?

00:02:34.996 --> 00:02:36.570
- [Floyd] Dang man.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.433
- [Policeman] Put your
hands on top of your head.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.770
- [Narrator] Chauvin's
attorney has previously argued

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.650
that police body camera
footage shows Floyd struggling

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.793
and actively resisting arrest.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.140
- [Policeman] Stop resisting then.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.160
- [Floyd] I'm not.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.150
- [Policeman] Yes you are.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.010
- [Narrator] The defense
has also pointed to footage

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.650
of Floyd saying he couldn't breathe

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.590
before he was ever on the ground,

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.730
as evidence that serious medical issues

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.130
were the cause of his death.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.383
- [Floyd] I can't
breath! (mumbles) Please!

00:03:07.648 --> 00:03:09.050
(somber music)

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:09.960
- [Narrator] Medical evidence,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.600
will also feature prominently in the trial

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.200
but the jury may hear
conflicting testimony

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.810
especially because two
different autopsies determined

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.540
that Floyd's death was a homicide

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.873
but gave different reasons for his death.

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:25.180
- The defense only wants to
have the official medical

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.500
examiners report,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.030
which talks about George Floyd dying

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.760
because of cardiac arrest,
complicated by the police

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.100
trying to arrest him and subdue him.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.710
And then also mentioning
that he had fentanyl

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.713
and methamphetamines
and a heart condition.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.670
- [Narrator] Chauvin's attorney
has pointed to that autopsy

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.570
to argue that Floyd couldn't
breathe due to a lethal dose

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.050
of Fentanyl and possibly a speedball,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.270
and that other pre-existing
conditions combined

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.540
with the drugs most
likely led to his death.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.080
But another autopsy
prepared for Floyd's family

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.130
came to a different conclusion,

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.103
that he died of asphyxiation
at the hands of the police.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.038
- Well, I think the question
the two sides are gonna be

00:04:06.038 --> 00:04:07.610
going back and forth on is,

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.965
what's standard procedure
and what is police training.

00:04:10.965 --> 00:04:13.500
- So what is the bar for a conviction?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.850
The most serious charges Chauvin is facing

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.440
is, second degree unintentional murder.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.850
That means prosecutors don't
need to prove intent to kill

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.530
but they must prove beyond a
reasonable doubt that Chauvin

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.040
intentionally inflicted
substantial bodily harm to Floyd,

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.420
which resulted in Floyd's death.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.770
- What they are actually
trying to prove is that

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.370
in the course of him being
assaulted by the police, he died.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.440
So the police didn't intend to kill him

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.820
but by assaulting him,
it led to his death.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.010
- [Narrator] Chauvin's attorney argued

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.240
that the officers
properly restrained Floyd

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.730
because he was acting
erratically and posed a risk

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.523
of injury to himself and
potentially to the officers.

00:04:54.523 --> 00:04:57.510
- It really is a difficult
thing to come to grips with

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.604
because everybody's seen this video

00:04:59.604 --> 00:05:02.650
the officers were all fired immediately

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.210
but it's still very difficult

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.385
to prosecute a police
officer in this country.

00:05:07.385 --> 00:05:09.900
- [Narrator] Legally, the stakes are high.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.320
If Chauvin is acquitted,

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.160
the three other officers
may not go to trial at all

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.800
for aiding and abetting him.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.870
Currently, that trial has
been delayed until August.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:19.970
Lawyers for the three,

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.714
have made filings indicating
they aren't guilty.

00:05:22.714 --> 00:05:25.297
(somber music)

